WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.246
String construction often combines
formatting, appending, allocation,

2
00:00:04.326 --> 00:00:05.158
and validation.

3
00:00:05.238 --> 00:00:07.200
That makes it easy to hide assumptions.

4
00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:10.848
A format string should be a trusted
template controlled by the program.

5
00:00:10.928 --> 00:00:12.949
Untrusted text should be passed as data,

6
00:00:13.029 --> 00:00:16.656
not used as the format that tells the
library how to interpret arguments.

7
00:00:16.736 --> 00:00:19.412
Capacity-aware formatted output should be
checked.

8
00:00:19.492 --> 00:00:22.881
The result may indicate failure,
truncation, or the required size.

9
00:00:22.961 --> 00:00:26.766
If the formatted string becomes a path,
configuration key, protocol field,

10
00:00:26.846 --> 00:00:30.117
log line, or command argument for an
approved process interface,

11
00:00:30.197 --> 00:00:31.980
partial output can change meaning.

12
00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:35.093
The code should decide whether to retry
with a larger buffer,

13
00:00:35.173 --> 00:00:37.432
reject the operation, or use a safe
fallback.

14
00:00:37.512 --> 00:00:41.020
Builder-style abstractions can make
complex construction cleaner.

15
00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:44.727
A good builder reports errors clearly,
tracks length, owns its storage,

16
00:00:44.807 --> 00:00:46.591
and has predictable cleanup rules.

17
00:00:46.671 --> 00:00:50.892
It should also expose whether the final
string is complete and terminated according

18
00:00:50.972 --> 00:00:52.221
to the library contract.

19
00:00:52.301 --> 00:00:54.858
Append operations need length and capacity
rules.

20
00:00:54.938 --> 00:00:58.029
Appending a C string is different from
appending a byte range.

21
00:00:58.109 --> 00:01:02.093
Appending formatted text is different from
appending already validated text.

22
00:01:02.173 --> 00:01:06.157
The API should make those differences
visible so the caller can choose the right

23
00:01:06.237 --> 00:01:06.831
operation.

24
00:01:06.911 --> 00:01:09.349
Constructed strings still require
validation.

25
00:01:09.429 --> 00:01:12.343
A path assembled safely is not
automatically authorized.

26
00:01:12.423 --> 00:01:15.931
A message built without truncation is not
automatically meaningful.

27
00:01:16.011 --> 00:01:19.875
A formatted value still needs the
application rules for allowed characters,

28
00:01:19.955 --> 00:01:21.561
length, encoding, and purpose.

29
00:01:21.641 --> 00:01:24.495
Safer construction helps maintain the
representation;

30
00:01:24.575 --> 00:01:26.180
it does not replace validation.
